why 2 0 CROSSING The melody lies low here, so all voices crowd together. The alto holds A while the tenor walks down D–C–B–A past it, above the alto for one beat.
why 2 1/8 CROSSING The tenor keeps walking down through C toward the alto's A.
why 2 1/4 CROSSING The last step of the tenor's descent, B, before it meets the alto's A in unison.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING The tenor holds its A as a common tone while the alto leaps up to A to fill out the chord higher. The doubled third keeps the held note.
why 5 0 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices fall into ii°⁶ as the phrase begins its descent. The bass's leap A–D keeps the motion strong, and the lines spread apart again on the next beat.
why 5 3/4 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both leap up, C–F and A–D, to place the chord higher. They move together, so the voices stay in order.
why 7 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All four voices step down together into a passing diminished seventh chord heading for D minor, led by the bass's chromatic E–D–C♯–D.
why 7 1/2 LEADING_TONE The E here is a suspended note over D minor, not a leading tone. It falls to D, as a suspension should.
why 9 0 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) both move by step onto B, the third of the G chord. Bach takes the nearest notes.
why 9 3/4 DOUBLING The same move as on the downbeat, in sequence: soprano and tenor step onto B together.
why 11 1/2 CROSSING The melody dips down to D, and the alto keeps its own line F–E above it instead of jumping below. For a beat the alto is on top.
why 11 5/8 CROSSING The alto steps down F–E over the soprano's D; then the soprano leaps up to G and is on top again.
why 12 1/8 LEAPS In the last bar's decoration the alto falls F–E–D and skips to B. Its ends form a tritone, but the line moves mostly by step.
why 12 1/2 SEVENTH The last bar is one long fermata over a bass walking C–B–A–E. The notes above it decorate the cadence onto E major; they are not sevenths that must resolve.
why 12 1/2 CADENCE The melody is in the Phrygian mode and ends on E, so Bach ends on the E major chord, which in A minor is V. This Phrygian ending is common in chorales.
